## Boot Log Collection Process

1. Configure Raspberry Pi boot environment.
2. Connect UART adapter to Raspberry Pi GPIO serial pins.
3. Introduce a controlled fault into the boot process.
4. Power-cycle the device.
5. Capture boot logs through UART.
6. Store collected logs under the corresponding failure category.
7. Label logs with failure type and affected boot stage.

---

## Fault Injection Matrix

| ID | Failure Scenario | Injection Method | Expected Stage | Purpose |
|----|------------------|------------------|----------------|---------|
| E01 | Missing Root Filesystem | Incorrect root= parameter | Kernel | RootFS mount failure |
| E02 | Invalid Boot Arguments | Wrong bootargs passed by U-Boot | Kernel | Boot parameter validation |
| E03 | Missing init Process | Remove `/init` from BusyBox RootFS | Userspace | Startup failure |
| E05 | Missing Device Tree Blob (DTB) | Remove DTB file from boot partition | U-Boot / Kernel | Hardware description failure |
| E06 | Invalid DTB Reference | Wrong DTB filename in boot config | U-Boot | Boot configuration failure |
| E07 | Incorrect Kernel Image | Replace kernel image with invalid file | Kernel | Kernel load failure |
| E08 | Firmware Configuration Error | Modify `config.txt` parameters | Firmware | Early boot anomaly |
| E09 | UART Console Misconfiguration | Invalid console boot argument | Kernel | Log collection failure testing |

